This is a major finding. `login.sipgate.com` (the real app login) runs **Apache Keycloak** (`/auth/realms/sipgate-apps/protocol/openid-connect`), using the **OAuth2 implicit flow** (`response_type=token`). This is the app's identity provider. Keycloak presents several HIGH-VALUE hypothesis classes:

1. **OAuth implicit flow** = tokens in URL fragment (redirect_uri / open redirect theft, token leakage via Referer/history).
2. **`redirect_uri=https://app.sipgate.com/implicit-auth-redirect?redirect=/`** — the app's redirect_uri carries a `redirect=` param. Potential **open-redirect chained to token theft** if the `/implicit-auth-redirect` endpoint trusts `redirect=/` to an external host.
3. Keycloak realm `sipgate-apps` — could test for Keycloak CVEs, but those are mostly auth-bypass/known-library which is borderline REJECTED without program-specific exploit.

The `x-frame-options: ALLOW-FROM chrome-extension://jaemdlgbgkndaldpkoacdhdiegopjofa/*` header is notable — allows framing by a specific Chrome extension ID. That's a clickjacking/IFrame configuration on the login page. Actually ALLOW-FROM is deprecated/non-standard, but the intent is to permit framing by that extension. Not a vuln per se (clickjacking is REJECTED without demonstrated exploit).

Let me probe the `/implicit-auth-redirect` endpoint to understand the `redirect=` handling — the key OAuth chain candidate. Also enumerate the Keycloak realm read-only.

Let me check the redirect target handling on app.sipgate.com/implicit-auth-redirect.
[0m$ [0mecho "=== implicit-auth-redirect empty ==="; curl -s -o /dev/null -D - --max-time 12 -A "Mozilla/5.0" "https://app.sipgate.com/implicit-auth-redirect" | grep -iE "HTTP/|location|server" | head -5; echo "=== with external redirect param ==="; curl -s -o /dev/null -D - --max-time 12 -A "Mozilla/5.0" "https://app.sipgate.com/implicit-auth-redirect?redirect=https://evil.example" | grep -iE "HTTP/|location" | head -5
